From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dawid Wesierski <dawid.wesierski@intel.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] eal/windows: fix use after free in alarm cancel

rte_eal_alarm_cancel() walks the alarm list with LIST_FOREACH() and
calls alarm_remove_unsafe() on each armed entry that matches.
alarm_remove_unsafe() frees the entry, and the next step of
LIST_FOREACH() then reads the next pointer from freed memory. Each
cancel that removes an entry does this, and the process can crash.

The net/iavf PMD cancels alarms in normal operation: its watchdog on a
link up event, and its device alarm on close and on the error path of
iavf_dev_init(). On Windows, a VF that failed its init stopped the
whole process with a segmentation fault.

Use LIST_FOREACH_SAFE(), which reads the next entry first.

Fixes: f4cbdbc7fbd2 ("eal/windows: implement alarm API")
Cc: stable@dpdk.org

Signed-off-by: Dawid Wesierski <dawid.wesierski@intel.com>
---
 lib/eal/windows/eal_alarm.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/eal/windows/eal_alarm.c b/lib/eal/windows/eal_alarm.c
index 0b11d331dc..ed6e7f2245 100644
--- a/lib/eal/windows/eal_alarm.c
+++ b/lib/eal/windows/eal_alarm.c
@@ -190,7 +190,7 @@ RTE_EXPORT_SYMBOL(rte_eal_alarm_cancel)
 int
 rte_eal_alarm_cancel(rte_eal_alarm_callback cb_fn, void *cb_arg)
 {
-	struct alarm_entry *ap;
+	struct alarm_entry *ap, *ap_next;
 	unsigned int state;
 	int removed;
 	bool executing;
@@ -207,7 +207,7 @@ rte_eal_alarm_cancel(rte_eal_alarm_callback cb_fn, void *cb_arg)
 
 		rte_spinlock_lock(&alarm_lock);
 
-		LIST_FOREACH(ap, &alarm_list, next) {
+		LIST_FOREACH_SAFE(ap, &alarm_list, next, ap_next) {
 			if (!alarm_matches(ap, cb_fn, cb_arg))
 				continue;
 
